// Package `core` — Direct-manipulation hints (core/src/builtins/hints.rs,
// docs/proposals/dm-v2-hints.md).
//
// Both return their first argument unchanged. Tools read them from the
// program: `tunable` marks the literal `value` resolves to as a preferred
// target for a drag, optionally with a range and a step, or every number in
// it when `value` is data (a record, a list); `options:` lists the values a
// setting can take. A top-level `let X = tunable(…)` is a setting that hosts
// list and set. `rigid` marks a literal as never moving, and stops feedback
// from passing through the call. Neither name can be redefined.
// See docs/Builtins.md (Direct-Manipulation Hints).

pub native fn tunable(value, min?: num, max?: num, step?: num, options?: list)
// The form a setting with choices uses: `tunable("title", options: [...])`.
pub native fn tunable(value, options: list)
pub native fn rigid(value)

// `const(e)`: `e`, which must be static. Around the whole initializer of a
// top-level `let` it is the `const` declaration: `let X = const(e)` means
// `const X = e` (docs/tasks/lifecycle-annotation-functions-refactor.md). The
// compiler erases the call. The name cannot be redefined.
pub native fn const(value)
